  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\n   - We are to find an improved upper bound for the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem. The current best known value is ~0.3808. The optimization problem involves minimizing the maximal overlap integral \\(C_5 = \\max_k \\int_0^2 h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\\) for a step function \\(h: [0,2] \\to [0,1]\\) with integral exactly 1.\n   - The previous algorithm used a hybrid of gradient-free local search (SLSQP) and global explorers like random perturbations and structured initial guesses. However, these methods struggled to consistently break 0.3808.\n   - We propose to use Bayesian optimization as the primary method, leveraging its ability to balance exploration and exploitation effectively, combined with dimensionality reduction via active subspace analysis.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n   - The decision variables are the values of \\(h\\) at \\(n\\) grid points, subject to:\n        \\(0 \\leq h[i] \\leq 1\\) for all \\(i\\) (explicit clipping)\n        \\(\\sum h[i] = n/2\\) (exact with clipping and adjustment)\n   - The objective function is:\n        \\(f(h) = \\max_k \\text{correlation\\_mode\\_full}(h, 1-h, \\text{mode}=\\text{\"full\"}) \\cdot (2/n)\\)\n   - Bayesian optimization requires:\n        Lower confidence bound (LCB) acquisition function\n        Gradient-based surrogate model (Gaussian process)\n        Expected improvement for parallel evaluations\n   - Active subspace analysis will reduce dimensionality by identifying the most influential directions. The optimization proceeds by:\n        1) Identifying direction vectors\n        2) Projecting variables onto the active subspace\n        3) Performing dimensionality-reduced Bayesian optimization\n        4) Synthesizing full-dimensional optimum\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n   - **Parent validation**: If initial_h_values available, confirm feasibility using project_feasible.\n   - **Initialization**:\n        - Let \\(n_0 = \\min(\\text{len}(initial\\_h\\_values), 300\\)) as starting grid (even with clipping)\n        - Generate random initial points in sampled grid\n        - Evaluate with LCB acquisition function\n   - **Loop until time exceeds budget**:\n        - Evaluate maxima distribution\n        - Compute active subspace using gradient samples\n        - Project variables into low-dimensional active manifold\n        - Perform Bayesian optimization in projected space:\n            - Train Gaussian process surrogate\n            - Optimize LCB acquisition function\n            - Select next point for full-dimensional evaluation\n        - Project back and enforce box constraints explicitly\n        - Update best solution found so far\n   - **Fallback**: If any projected dimension fails, use a multi-start grid search for neighbors\n   - **Termination**:\n        - Return best solution within time budget\n        - If time exhausted, return best found solution\n\n4. Search choices and hyperparameters\n   - Active subspace projections with 5-dimensional target canopy\n   - Gaussian process surrogate with automatic relevance detection\n   - LCB parameter = 3 (exploration focus)\n   - Initial grid = 5 points per dimension for subset search\n   - Evaluate every iteration: 0.97 success rate depending on initial sampling\n\n5. Computational budget and robustness\n   - Time budget distribution:\n        - Active subspace computation: 5%\n        - Projected BO: 15%\n        - Dimension restoration: 10%\n        - Global exploration: 60%\n        - Fallback/repair: 10%\n   - Estimation: Latin hypercube sampling for initial distribution\n   - Terminate if no improvement in 20 iterations\n   - Budget check: Compute maximum allowed iterations = 1300 / (current_benchmark * 0.99)\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n   - Enforce bounds with explicit clipping after projection\n   - Maintain sum constraint via projected_feasible()\n   - Re-evaluate candidate via compute_c5()\n   - Check finite values and before return\n   - Ensure grid dimensions align when n changes\n   - Finally, return (h_values, bounding_value, n) using project_feasible()",
